The proof of the Brouwer fixed point theorem involves advanced mathematical concepts, including the notion of the degree of a map. The degree of a map is a way to describe how the map wraps the sphere around itself. For any continuous map from the sphere to itself, the degree must be an integer. The key insight of the proof is that if a map has no fixed points, then its degree must be zero. However, it can be shown that the identity map (which maps every point to itself) has a degree of 1, not 0. This implies that any continuous map from the sphere to itself must have at least one fixed point.

